          <a href="#process" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. The Process Start to Finish</a>
          <a href="#one-way" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Why This Is a One-Way Door</a>
          <a href="#alternatives" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Should You Paint At All?</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Paint needs something to hold on to. A solid stain gives it a film; a penetrating stain gives it a water-repellent surface engineered to shed exactly this kind of liquid. Almost every peeling deck-paint job traces back to that distinction being ignored.
        </p>

        <div id="which-stain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Which Stain Is On the Deck
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Existing Finish</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Can You Paint It?</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Needs</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solid stain, sound</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean, scuff sand, bonding primer</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solid stain, peeling</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not yet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strip or scrape back to sound, then prime</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Semi-transparent, water-based</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Marginal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strip, or sand heavily plus bonding primer</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Semi-transparent, oil-based</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not without stripping</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strip, brighten, oil-based bonding primer</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clear sealer or water repellent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strip and weather until water soaks in</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fully weathered, bare grey wood</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean, brighten, dry, prime</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you do not know what was applied — a common situation with a house bought two owners ago — the tests in the next section will tell you what matters, which is not the brand but whether the finish is a film and whether it still repels water.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tests" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Two Tests That Settle It
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Test One: Water</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Sprinkle water on several boards. Beading means a penetrating finish is still active and paint will not stick.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Test the walkways, the sheltered edges and the area under the furniture separately — it is entirely normal for one part of a deck to be worn bare while another is still fully sealed, and that unevenness is itself a reason to strip.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The second test is the one that actually saves the job, and almost nobody does it. Prepare a small area exactly as you intend to prepare the deck — clean it, scuff it, prime it and paint it — then leave it a full week to cure properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            After that week, press a strip of strong packing tape hard onto the painted patch, run your thumbnail along it to make sure it is fully in contact, and rip it off sharply. Paint coming away on the tape means the whole deck will do the same, just more slowly and after you have spent $200 on paint. Paint staying put means your preparation is adequate.
          </p>

          <p>
            An hour of work and a week of waiting is a cheap price for knowing. Skipping it is how people end up scraping a deck they painted the previous spring.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="solid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Painting Over a Solid Stain
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the easy case, because a solid-colour stain is essentially a thin paint already. It sits on the surface, it is opaque, and if it is well bonded it makes a perfectly reasonable substrate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wash the deck thoroughly and let it dry. Scuff sand with 80 to 120 grit to dull the sheen and knock back any raised edges. Scrape or sand any area that is flaking back to a sound, feathered edge — you cannot paint over a lifting edge and expect it to stop lifting. Then prime with an exterior latex bonding primer and apply two thin coats of acrylic porch and floor paint.
          </p>

          <p>
            One caution: solid stain and paint on a deck floor share the same fundamental weakness. Both are films on a horizontal surface that collects water, and both eventually fail by peeling. Adding a coat of paint over a solid stain builds total film thickness, and thicker films crack sooner. Two thin coats, not three generous ones.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="penetrating" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Painting Over a Penetrating Stain
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is where the trouble is. A penetrating stain, especially an oil-based one, works by leaving the wood water-repellent — and paint is delivered in water. Applying latex paint to a surface designed to reject water gives you a coat that dries, looks acceptable for a season, and then sheets off in the first hard freeze.
          </p>

          <p>
            The real fix is stripping. A stain stripper, applied and dwelled properly, followed by a neutralising brightener and a full drying period, takes the deck back to a surface that will accept a coating. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-strip-deck-stain/">stripping deck stain</a> covers the products and the process, and it is genuinely a full day's work on an average deck.
          </p>

          <p>
            The alternative is time. A deck left unfinished for two or three seasons loses its repellency naturally, and once water soaks in everywhere rather than beading anywhere, it will take paint. That is not a plan so much as an observation about decks that have already been neglected.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where some oil residue is unavoidable, an oil-based or shellac-based exterior bonding primer gives the best odds available. It is not as good as stripping, and it is a great deal better than latex primer straight onto an oiled surface.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="primer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Choosing Primer and Paint
          </h2>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Over a sound solid stain:</strong> exterior latex bonding primer, then 100% acrylic porch and floor paint.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Over residual oil stain:</strong> oil-based or shellac-based exterior bonding primer, then acrylic topcoats.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>On cedar or redwood:</strong> a stain-blocking primer regardless, or tannin will bleed brown through a pale topcoat within months.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Avoid thick resurfacer coatings</strong> on a deck floor. They bridge gaps, trap moisture underneath and peel in large sheets rather than small flakes.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Avoid ordinary exterior wall paint.</strong> It is not formulated for foot traffic or standing water, and it will not last a season on a deck floor.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Consider a solid stain instead of paint.</strong> It carries less film for the same opacity, breathes better, and fails less dramatically when it eventually does.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-deck-paint/">deck paint picks</a> cover the acrylics that hold up on horizontal surfaces, and <a href="/best-porch-paint/">porch paints</a> covers the closely related case of a covered porch floor, where the odds are considerably better.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="process" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Process Start to Finish
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Day One — Clean and Strip</h3>
          <p>
            Wash the deck with a percarbonate cleaner, and strip it if the tests said so. Neutralise with a brightener, rinse hard, and stop for the day. The method is in <a href="/how-to-clean-a-deck/">how to clean a deck</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Day Two — Wait</h3>
          <p>
            The deck has to be genuinely dry, not surface dry. Two days is typical, up to four in humidity or heavy shade. Use the water-drop test rather than judging by eye, and use the time to set popped nails and replace any board that is past saving.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Day Three — Sand and Prime</h3>
          <p>
            Sand with 80 grit to knock back raised grain and give the primer a key, then vacuum and wipe every trace of dust. Prime the whole deck, including board ends and the underside of the railing cap where water sits. Give the primer its full recoat window.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Day Four — Two Thin Coats</h3>
          <p>
            Work two or three boards at a time along their full length to avoid lap marks. Two thin coats bond far better than one thick one, and thin coats are also what keeps total film thickness down. Watch the forecast: most acrylics need 24 hours clear of rain and the same clear of overnight dew.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Then Wait a Week</h3>
          <p>
            Foot traffic after 24 to 48 hours, but keep furniture off for a full week. Chair feet on a partly cured film leave permanent dents and start the first peel, invariably in the most visible place on the deck.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="one-way" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Why This Is a One-Way Door
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once a deck is painted, it is painted. Going back to a semi-transparent stain means removing every trace of paint from the grain, and paint sits in the grain far more stubbornly than stain ever does. Even a thorough strip-and-sand typically leaves enough behind to make a transparent finish look mottled.
          </p>

          <p>
            The recurring cost is worth stating plainly too. A painted deck floor lasts four to seven years, which is longer than most stains — but when it fails, it fails by peeling, and the next job is scraping and sanding rather than washing and recoating. Penetrating stains cost more attention up front and less at the end; paint reverses that.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of which is an argument against painting a deck that should be painted. It is an argument for being sure, because the decision does not unwind.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Should You Paint At All?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The strongest cases for painting a deck are cosmetic and structural rather than protective: mismatched boards after repairs, a deck that has already been painted by a previous owner, a low-grade lumber deck whose grain is not worth showing, or a colour scheme that demands it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The strongest cases against are a deck that currently carries a penetrating stain and is performing fine, a deck in a wet or heavily freeze-thawed climate where films struggle, and a deck whose owner would rather wash and recoat every few years than scrape one day.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the goal is opacity rather than paint specifically, a solid-colour stain is the middle path — it hides the grain almost as completely, carries less film, and its eventual failure is gentler. The full comparison is in <a href="/deck-paint-vs-deck-stain/">deck paint versus deck stain</a>, and the opacity question in <a href="/solid-vs-semi-transparent-deck-stain/">solid versus semi-transparent stain</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Railings Are a Different Calculation</h2>
        <p>
          Everything above assumes the deck floor, which is the worst surface a paint film can be asked to live on: horizontal, walked over, and holding water every time it rains. Railings, posts, spindles and the fascia are a far friendlier proposition, and it is worth treating them as a separate decision rather than assuming one answer covers the whole structure.
        </p>
        <p>
          A vertical surface sheds water instead of pooling it, takes no foot traffic, and sees less direct midday sun. In practice that means a painted railing routinely lasts twice as long as the same paint on the same deck's floor — seven to ten years is entirely normal — and when it does fail, it tends to fail gradually at the top edges rather than sheeting off in the walkways.
        </p>
        <p>
          Plenty of decks are best served by painting the railings and staining the floor. The railings get the colour, the crisp lines and the long interval; the floor keeps a penetrating finish that wears away rather than peels, and never needs scraping. The prep rules do not change — the same water test, the same tape test, the same bonding primer — but the odds of the result lasting are considerably better on anything standing up.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting Straight Over an Oil Stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The old finish is still repelling water, and paint is water-borne. It will dry, look fine for a season and then sheet off. Either strip it or use an oil-based bonding primer — and test a patch either way before committing the deck.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Primer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Self-priming paints assume a friendly substrate, and an old stained deck is not one. Primer is what bonds to the awkward surface and blocks tannin bleed on cedar and redwood. It is the cheapest insurance in the whole job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting Over Peeling Areas</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A lifting edge under fresh paint is still a lifting edge, and it takes the new coat with it. Scrape back to sound material, feather the edges with sandpaper, and spot-prime before the deck gets its full coats.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">One Thick Coat Instead of Two Thin Ones</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thick film on a horizontal surface cracks sooner and peels in larger pieces. Two thin coats bond better, cure properly and add less total thickness over the solid stain that may already be underneath.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Replacing Furniture After Two Days</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Walkable is not cured. Acrylic films continue hardening for a week or more, and chair feet on a soft film leave dents that become the first failure point. Keep everything off the deck for seven days.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint over deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the stain. Paint goes over a sound solid-colour stain readily, since that is already a film to bond to. Over a penetrating semi-transparent or transparent stain — especially oil-based — paint has nothing to grip and will peel, so the old finish must be stripped or fully weathered first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you have to strip deck stain before painting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not over a sound solid stain — a clean and a scuff sand is enough. You do need to strip a penetrating oil stain, any peeling area, and anything that fails a tape test. Painting over a failing finish just transfers the failure to the new coat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint over oil-based deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only after stripping, or after the deck has weathered enough that water no longer beads. Oil stains leave a repellent, slightly waxy surface that latex paint cannot bond to. Where residual oil is unavoidable, an oil-based or shellac-based bonding primer gives the best available odds.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you know if the old stain will hold paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tape test. Clean, scuff, prime and paint a small area, cure it a full week, then press strong tape onto it and pull sharply. Paint on the tape means the deck will fail. Also sprinkle water on bare-looking areas — beading means a penetrating finish is still present.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What kind of paint should you use on a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 100% acrylic exterior porch and floor paint, or a dedicated acrylic deck coating. Acrylic stays flexible enough to move with the boards. Avoid interior paint, ordinary exterior wall paint and thick resurfacers, which trap moisture and peel in sheets on a deck floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need primer to paint over deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In nearly every case. Exterior latex bonding primer over a sound solid stain; oil-based or shellac-based bonding primer where oil stain remains. Primer also blocks the tannin bleed that otherwise shows through a pale topcoat on cedar and redwood within months.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it better to paint or restain a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a deck already carrying a penetrating stain, restaining is easier and more durable — a wash rather than a strip, and it wears away instead of peeling. Paint makes more sense where boards are already mismatched or previously painted and hiding the surface is the goal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you go back to stain after painting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only by stripping and sanding off every trace of paint, which on a deck floor is a large job and rarely fully successful — paint left in the grain shows through any semi-transparent finish. Treat painting a deck as a one-way decision, because in practice it is.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

