        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Solid stain: yes, after a clean, a scuff and a bonding primer.</strong> Penetrating stain, especially oil-based: not until it has been stripped or has fully weathered away. The old finish's job was to repel water, and paint arrives as water.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Two tests decide it. Sprinkle water on several boards — beading means a penetrating finish is still working. Then paint a test patch exactly as you plan to do the deck, cure it a full week, and pull tape off it. Those two hours are worth more than any product recommendation.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            And be certain before you start, because paint on a deck does not unwind. If what you actually want is opacity rather than paint, a solid-colour stain gets you most of the way there with a thinner film and a far gentler failure.
          </p>
        </div>


